The problem asks us to find the number of pairs $(i, j)$ with $i < j$ such that $b_i^{b_j} = b_j^{b_i}$, where $b_k = 2^{a_k}$.
The input gives us an array $a$ of length $n$, where $a_i$ are integers.
The condition is $b_i^{b_j} = b_j^{b_i}$.
Substituting $b_k = 2^{a_k}$, we get $(2^{a_i})^{(2^{a_j})} = (2^{a_j})^{(2^{a_i})}$.
This simplifies to $2^{a_i \cdot 2^{a_j}} = 2^{a_j \cdot 2^{a_i}}$.
Since the base is 2 (which is not 0 or 1), the exponents must be equal.
